1. Remove the localhost restriction from the proxy.
   Website visitors do not appear as localhost when HaskellHost runs in Docker or
   on a remote server, so legitimate frontend requests must be accepted.

2. Add an API endpoint whitelist to the configuration.
   Associate secrets with the hosts that may receive them, and reject proxy URLs
   that attempt to interpolate a secret for any unapproved destination.

3. Add rate limiting to the proxy endpoint.
   Limit requests per client IP over a configurable time window so attackers
   cannot freely consume an approved API's quota through HaskellHost.
